"""Original shock questions: distinguish mechanisms and reversible causes."""
from create_critical_hemodynamics import rows,q,HE,SS,CS,ref
PE=ref('AHA/ACC multisociety acute pulmonary embolism guideline: 2026','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jacc.2025.11.005')
PC=ref('ESC myocarditis/pericarditis guideline: 2025','https://doi.org/10.1093/eurheartj/ehaf192')

q('A patient with major GI bleeding has a small hyperdynamic LV, low filling pressures and cool extremities. Which primary shock mechanism best fits?', 'Hypovolemic shock',[
 ('Primary LV cardiogenic shock','This more typically has pump dysfunction and elevated left-sided filling pressure.'),('Cardiac tamponade','A pericardial obstructive pattern would need supporting evidence.'),('Isolated distributive vasoplegia','Vasoplegia may coexist, but the blood loss and low-preload phenotype make hypovolemia the primary mechanism.')],
 'Link the circulation to the loss mechanism','**Acute blood loss reduces effective preload and stroke volume.**',['Compensatory tachycardia and vasoconstriction can initially preserve pressure.','The hemoglobin concentration may not immediately reflect the full magnitude of acute loss.'], '**Control bleeding and restore appropriate circulating volume and oxygen-carrying capacity.**',['Use a major-hemorrhage strategy when indicated.','Avoid treating the pressure alone while ongoing blood loss continues.'],[['Volume loss','Low effective preload'],['Compensation','Tachycardia/vasoconstriction'],['Definitive priority','Hemorrhage control']],[HE],style='clinical_scenario')

q('A febrile patient has warm extremities, low diastolic pressure, high cardiac output and low calculated SVR despite hypotension. Which mechanism predominates?', 'Distributive vasoplegia',[
 ('Pure LV pump failure','That usually has low forward output rather than the stated high-output low-resistance pattern.'),('Pure hypovolemia without vasodilation','This more typically produces compensatory increased vascular resistance.'),('Mechanical right-heart inflow obstruction','This requires an obstructive loading pattern rather than this primary vasodilatory circulation.')],
 'High flow does not guarantee adequate tissue distribution','**Distributive shock can have low resistance and high or normal cardiac output.**',['Microcirculatory dysfunction and relative hypovolemia can coexist.','Late or mixed septic shock may also have myocardial depression and low output.'], '**Treat the cause and restore effective perfusion.**',['Use appropriate fluids based on reassessment and vasopressor support when needed.','Do not assume every septic patient has the same cardiac-output phenotype.'],[['Resistance','Low'],['Output','Can be high initially'],['Mixed disease','May add pump dysfunction']],[HE,SS],style='clinical_scenario')

q('A patient has hypotension, elevated jugular pressure and echo evidence of a pericardial effusion with right-sided chamber collapse in a compatible clinical setting. Which intervention addresses the primary mechanism?', 'Urgent pericardial drainage',[
 ('Large-dose loop diuretic as the defining initial treatment','Reducing preload can worsen tamponade physiology.'),('Thrombolysis as the default treatment','This is not indicated by a pericardial compression mechanism.'),('A beta-agonist infusion as definitive treatment without drainage','Temporary support does not relieve the mechanical compression.')],
 'Relieve the obstruction to cardiac filling','**Tamponade is a clinical-hemodynamic syndrome caused by pericardial pressure impairing filling.**',['Effusion size alone does not define severity.','Positive-pressure ventilation and induction can worsen venous return in a precarious patient.'], '**Arrange expert drainage while supporting perfusion.**',['The route depends on cause and anatomy.','Trauma, purulent disease or other settings may need a surgical approach.'],[['Mechanism','Impaired filling'],['Support','Bridge to source correction'],['Definitive action','Drainage']],[PC],style='clinical_scenario')

q('A ventilated patient abruptly becomes hypotensive and hypoxemic after a procedure. Peak pressures rise and breath sounds are absent on one side. Tension pneumothorax is strongly suspected. What is the priority?', 'Immediate decompression without waiting for a chest radiograph',[
 ('Transport for CT before intervention','This delays correction of a potentially fatal bedside diagnosis.'),('Increase PEEP to recruit the affected side','This can worsen the pressure-related obstruction.'),('Give a diuretic for presumed edema','This does not relieve intrapleural tension.')],
 'Correct a time-critical mechanical obstruction','**Tension pneumothorax can impair venous return and cause arrest.**',['The diagnosis is clinical in an unstable compatible setting.','Ultrasound can assist if it does not delay decompression.'], '**Use an appropriate emergency decompression technique followed by definitive pleural management.**',['Reassess ventilation and circulation immediately.','Review the cause and confirm subsequent drain function.'],[['Ventilation pressure','Can rise'],['Circulation','Venous return impaired'],['Priority','Decompress']],[ref('RCUK 2025 special circumstances resuscitation guideline','https://www.resus.org.uk/professional-library/2025-resuscitation-guidelines/special-circumstances-guidelines')],style='clinical_scenario')

q('A postoperative patient develops abrupt dyspnea, persistent hypotension and marked RV dilation with septal flattening. LV filling is reduced. Which shock mechanism is most likely?', 'Acute pulmonary embolism causing RV pressure overload and obstructive shock',[
 ('Primary isolated LV infarction','The stated dominant acute RV pressure-loading pattern suggests another mechanism.'),('Pure hypovolemia','This would not ordinarily cause severe RV pressure overload and septal flattening.'),('Isolated neurogenic vasodilation','This does not explain the acute RV loading pattern.')],
 'A failing RV can obstruct effective LV filling','**High-risk PE can abruptly increase RV afterload and reduce systemic output.**',['Echo findings are not uniquely specific, so the clinical probability and alternative causes matter.','Severe instability may limit transport and require a bedside diagnostic/reperfusion pathway.'], '**Support perfusion and urgently assess reperfusion options.**',['Systemic thrombolysis, catheter or surgical approaches depend on risk, contraindications and resources.','Avoid repeated large fluid loads that further distend a pressure-overloaded RV.'],[['RV afterload','Abrupt increase'],['LV preload','Can fall'],['Definitive care','Risk-based reperfusion']],[PE],style='clinical_scenario')

q('Following acute MI, a patient has cool skin, pulmonary edema, low cardiac output and elevated left-sided filling pressure. Which primary shock mechanism best fits?', 'LV-predominant cardiogenic shock',[
 ('Pure hypovolemic shock','The pulmonary congestion/high filling-pressure pattern argues against isolated volume depletion.'),('Pure distributive shock','Vasodilation can coexist but does not explain the dominant low-output congested LV phenotype.'),('Isolated tension pneumothorax','That requires a different respiratory and obstructive pattern.')],
 'Recognize pump failure with backward congestion','**LV failure can reduce forward flow while raising pulmonary venous pressure.**',['Blood pressure alone can underestimate severity.','Mechanical complications after MI must be considered, especially with a new murmur or abrupt deterioration.'], '**Treat the ischemic cause and restore perfusion while avoiding unnecessary fluid loading.**',['Use urgent revascularization pathways when indicated.','Select vasoactive or mechanical support according to the phenotype and trajectory.'],[['Forward failure','Low output'],['Backward failure','Pulmonary congestion'],['Cause correction','Urgent ischemic/mechanical assessment']],[CS],style='clinical_scenario')

q('A patient with inferior STEMI has hypotension, elevated JVP, clear lungs and marked RV systolic dysfunction. Which phenotype is most likely?', 'RV-predominant infarction-related shock',[
 ('Isolated LV failure with severe pulmonary edema','The clear lungs and dominant RV dysfunction favor right-sided failure.'),('Pure hypovolemia without cardiac disease','This does not explain the raised venous pressure and acute RV infarct context.'),('Septic vasoplegia as established from low BP alone','The ischemic and echo findings provide a different direct mechanism.')],
 'Right-heart failure needs a preload-afterload-contractility assessment','**RV infarction can impair LV filling and systemic flow.**',['Avoid indiscriminate preload reduction.','A cautious fluid assessment may help selected underfilled patients, but congestion and RV overdistension can make more fluid harmful.'], '**Prioritize reperfusion and maintain coronary/systemic perfusion.**',['Review rhythm and AV synchrony.','Choose support without assuming every hypotensive RV patient needs liters of fluid.'],[['RV failure','High venous pressure/low forward flow'],['Lungs','Can remain clear'],['Fluid','Individualized rather than automatic']],[CS],style='clinical_scenario')

q('A septic patient receiving dobutamine becomes more hypotensive. Echo shows a small hypercontractile LV, systolic anterior mitral motion and a dynamic LV outflow gradient. Which mechanism explains the deterioration?', 'Dynamic LV outflow tract obstruction',[
 ('New severe global LV systolic depression','The hypercontractile small-cavity pattern is the opposite.'),('Acute pericardial tamponade','No pericardial compression pattern is described.'),('Primary severe pulmonary vascular obstruction','This would principally affect RV loading rather than the stated LV outflow pattern.')],
 'More contractility can sometimes reduce effective output','**Hypovolemia, low afterload and catecholamine drive can provoke dynamic LVOTO.**',['The gradient can worsen with inotropes.','Mitral regurgitation from systolic anterior motion can compound hemodynamic compromise.'], '**Reassess the inotrope and restore appropriate loading under expert guidance.**',['Selected patients need cautious preload/afterload correction and carefully chosen beta-blockade.','Do not apply the usual low-contractility shock algorithm without reviewing the echo.'],[['Small hyperdynamic LV','Predisposing pattern'],['Inotrope','Can worsen obstruction'],['Management','Mechanism-specific loading and drug review']],[HE,CS],style='clinical_scenario')

q('After a high cervical spinal injury, a patient has hypotension, relative bradycardia and warm skin. Bleeding is being actively excluded. Which mechanism is most consistent?', 'Neurogenic shock from loss of sympathetic vascular tone',[
 ('Pure hemorrhagic shock as established by bradycardia','Hemorrhage remains important to exclude, but this autonomic phenotype favors neurogenic vasodilation.'),('Isolated LV pump failure','The injury and autonomic pattern provide a more direct mechanism.'),('Cardiac tamponade as certain without supporting findings','That requires a different obstructive assessment.')],
 'Distinguish autonomic circulatory failure from spinal neurological areflexia','**Neurogenic shock reflects sympathetic loss with vasodilation and possible bradycardia.**',['Spinal shock describes neurological reflex changes and is not the same hemodynamic diagnosis.','Trauma patients can have simultaneous hemorrhage.'], '**Maintain spinal cord perfusion and correct circulatory instability.**',['Use appropriate volume assessment, vasopressors and bradycardia management.','Do not attribute all hypotension to the cord before excluding bleeding and other causes.'],[['Neurogenic shock','Autonomic hemodynamic failure'],['Spinal shock','Neurological reflex state'],['Trauma differential','Hemorrhage must be assessed']],[ref('Acute spinal cord injury hemodynamic management guideline: 2024','https://doi.org/10.1177/21925682231202348')],style='clinical_scenario')

q('Minutes after an IV antibiotic, an adult develops wheeze, generalized urticaria and hypotension while still having a pulse. Which initial medication is most appropriate?', 'Intramuscular epinephrine in the anterolateral thigh',[
 ('IV hydrocortisone as the first definitive circulatory rescue','Steroids do not act rapidly enough to replace epinephrine.'),('An H1 antihistamine as the primary shock treatment','This may help skin symptoms but does not replace epinephrine for airway/circulatory compromise.'),('A rapid undiluted IV epinephrine bolus as routine initial treatment in a patient with a pulse','This increases dosing/arrhythmia risk; refractory shock infusions require experienced monitored care.')],
 'Treat anaphylaxis promptly with the correct route','**IM epinephrine is first-line in anaphylaxis with a pulse.**',['Remove the trigger, call for help and assess airway, breathing and circulation.','Refractory cases can require IV fluids and a titrated IV epinephrine infusion in expert hands.'], '**Do not delay epinephrine for adjuncts or laboratory tests.**',['Position safely and avoid sudden standing.','Cardiac arrest uses the resuscitation algorithm rather than assuming the usual IM dose is sufficient.'],[['IM epinephrine','First-line with a pulse'],['Antihistamine/steroid','Adjunctive roles'],['Refractory shock','Monitored escalation']],[ref('RCUK anaphylaxis/special circumstances: 2025','https://www.resus.org.uk/professional-library/2025-resuscitation-guidelines/special-circumstances-guidelines')],style='clinical_scenario')

q('A patient with known adrenal insufficiency has vomiting, hypotension, hyponatremia and hyperkalemia after stopping replacement. Which immediate treatment principle is appropriate?', 'Prompt parenteral hydrocortisone and appropriate fluid resuscitation without waiting for confirmatory testing',[
 ('Wait for a morning cortisol before giving any glucocorticoid','A suspected crisis requires immediate treatment.'),('Treat only the potassium while withholding hormone replacement','Electrolyte treatment does not correct the cortisol deficiency.'),('Give a diuretic to correct hyponatremia as the defining intervention','This can worsen volume depletion and does not address the endocrine emergency.')],
 'Hormone replacement is time-critical in a suspected adrenal crisis','**Immediate IV or IM hydrocortisone is recommended.**',['An initial adult 100-mg dose is followed by stress replacement according to emergency guidance.','Treat dehydration, hypoglycemia and the precipitant as appropriate.'], '**Draw diagnostic samples first only if this does not delay care.**',['Monitor electrolytes and clinical response.','Plan sick-day education and ongoing replacement after stabilization.'],[['Crisis','Immediate parenteral replacement'],['Fluids','Restore perfusion appropriately'],['Cause','Treat precipitant and missed replacement']],[ref('NICE adrenal insufficiency emergency guidance','https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/NG243/chapter/recommendations')],style='clinical_scenario')

q('A septic patient with chronic LV dysfunction has both low SVR and depressed cardiac output with congestion. Which description is most appropriate?', 'Mixed distributive and cardiogenic shock',[
 ('Pure septic vasoplegia that rules out pump dysfunction','Sepsis and pre-existing disease can produce simultaneous pump failure.'),('Pure hypovolemia established solely by hypotension','Congestion and depressed function argue against that simple label.'),('A contradiction because shock mechanisms cannot coexist','Mixed shock is common in critical illness.')],
 'Do not force a complex circulation into one category','**More than one mechanism can contribute to the same patient’s shock.**',['Treatment that helps one component can worsen another, such as fluid loading in a congested low-output state.','Serial reassessment identifies changes during resuscitation.'], '**Treat the dominant reversible components and reassess the response.**',['Support vascular tone, evaluate contractility and use fluid only when beneficial and tolerated.','Treat infection and ischemic/mechanical contributors when present.'],[['Vasoplegia','Low resistance'],['Pump dysfunction','Low forward output'],['Mixed strategy','Component-directed support']],[HE,SS,CS],style='clinical_scenario')

if __name__=='__main__':print(f'Shock cumulative draft: {len(rows)} questions')
